WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.560
"One Piece" is one of the best-selling&nbsp;
manga series of all time and arguably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.320
one of the most popular pop culture&nbsp;
properties in the whole world. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.080
how did this pirate manga&nbsp;
become king of the stories?

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.400
Given the enormous success of both "One Piece"&nbsp;
and "Dragon Ball," it makes sense that Eiichiro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.680
Oda was inspired in part by what Akira Toriyama&nbsp;
had done with his series. The folks at the One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:28.440
Piece Podcast translated an interview between&nbsp;
Oda and "Naruto" writer Masashi Kishimoto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.720
in which the former called&nbsp;
Toriyama a "huge influence."

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.840
"I'd have to say that Toriyama-sensei&nbsp;
has been a huge influence. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.160
there's "Dragon Ball," but I&nbsp;
really loved "Dr. Slump," as well."

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.200
Although the rules of each universe are different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.240
the core elements of "One Piece," and the&nbsp;
rogue cast of characters at its center have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.640
a lot in common with the motley crew that Goku&nbsp;
assembles over the course of "Dragon Ball."

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.920
In a conversation with Toriyama, Oda&nbsp;
even acknowledged that he had fallen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.640
in love with "Dragon Ball" the second&nbsp;
he stumbled across it. Oda also said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.200
that there were specific things&nbsp;
about the art style in the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:05.000
that inspired him, including the way&nbsp;
that Toriyama drew muscles and hands.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.960
Although we could forgive Eiichiro Oda for&nbsp;
deciding to rest on his laurels after creating one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.280
of the most profitable stories in history, that's&nbsp;
not the path he chose. Instead, Oda continues to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.320
work a very intense schedule, and does a lot of&nbsp;
the drawing for "One Piece" himself. This process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:25.720
means that Oda is often working exceedingly long&nbsp;
days and suggests that he demands a lot of himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.120
in order to make sure that the stories in "One&nbsp;
Piece" are told the way he wants them to be.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:35.320
Oda reportedly wakes up at 5 a.m., and continues&nbsp;
working on through 2 a.m. the next day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:40.840
and rarely takes a day off, even on weekends.&nbsp;
"One Piece" became an enormous success in part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:45.080
because of Oda's attention to detail, and&nbsp;
it seems even now that it's world famous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:50.880
Oda still wants to make sure his story is told&nbsp;
with as much precision and care as possible.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.240
Oda initially thought that he would be able to&nbsp;
end the story of "One Piece" after five years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:00.080
according to a translated quote found on an&nbsp;
Amino anime blog. Although Oda knows how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.960
series will end, he realized it would take&nbsp;
him longer to get there than he had initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.760
suspected. He also acknowledged that he liked&nbsp;
the story too much to wrap it up that quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.880
and ultimately decided to keep it going&nbsp;
much longer than originally planned.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.640
In 2019, Oda said that he predicts "One Piece"&nbsp;
would end within the next five years, according&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.840
to Anime News Network. The series is on its way&nbsp;
toward a definitive conclusion, and at this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:27.360
that ending is still the one that Oda envisioned&nbsp;
when he initially planned to end the series after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.840
five years. Although the series will have run for&nbsp;
more than 25 years by the time it actually ends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:37.920
Oda is clearly committed to seeing his&nbsp;
vision for the manga's ending realized.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.720
On top of the movies, video games, and the anime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.840
"One Piece" finally got a live-action&nbsp;
adaptation in 2023, courtesy of Netflix.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.800
The live-action "One Piece" admittedly&nbsp;
caters to American fans of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.720
but it also works as part of Netflix's broader&nbsp;
push to reach a larger international audience.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.400
"One Piece" is a global property, and with Oda&nbsp;
serving as an executive producer on the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.960
fans around the world can be comfortable that&nbsp;
this new adaptation will continue to work as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.400
yet another part of the franchise. Plus,&nbsp;
with a second season officially greenlit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.840
fans have more of the live-action&nbsp;
adventure to look forward to.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.720
"That went pretty well… don't you think?"

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.520
Much like how "My Hero" was a pilot of sorts&nbsp;
for "My Hero Academia," "Romance Dawn" by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:25.920
Eiichiro Oda was a prototype for the "One Piece"&nbsp;
story. The first version of "Romance Dawn" was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:31.880
50-page one-shot published in the "Shōnen Jump&nbsp;
1996 Summer Special" on August 4th. The second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:37.280
version was only 45 pages long and released&nbsp;
in the 41st issue of "Weekly Shonen Jump" the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.800
following month. Both versions share similar&nbsp;
elements that are present in the finalized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:46.760
version of the manga while differing in many&nbsp;
ways. For example, Monkey D. Luffy is more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.040
concerned about going on fun adventures than&nbsp;
becoming king of the pirates. The two female&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.680
leads from the respective versions of "Romance&nbsp;
Dawn" — Silk in the first version and Ann in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:01.360
second version — are clear prototypes of Nami. The&nbsp;
Devil Fruits are said to come from a mystical tree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.840
in the second version of "Romance Dawn," while&nbsp;
their origin is still a mystery in "One Piece."

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:11.320
Red-Haired Shanks sacrifices his left arm to save&nbsp;
Luffy and gives Luffy his straw hat in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:15.960
version of the story; in the second version, Luffy&nbsp;
is inspired by his pirate grandfather who shares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:21.200
the same characteristics as Luffy's grandfather,&nbsp;
Monkey D. Garp, in "One Piece." "Romance Dawn" has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.840
been adapted in various ways. The first version&nbsp;
was adapted as an anime special called "One Piece:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:32.160
Romance Dawn Story." The second version&nbsp;
became Episode 907 of the TV anime series.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.000
While Monkey D. Luffy went through minimal&nbsp;
changes through the various prototypes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.320
"One Piece," the remaining members of the Straw&nbsp;
Hat Pirates went through various changes in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.960
initial development before becoming the pirates&nbsp;
they are today. The characters of Silk and Ann&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:50.000
from "Romance Dawn" paved the way for Nami in the&nbsp;
finalized story. However, according to the fourth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.520
databook, "One Piece Green: Secret Pieces,"&nbsp;
Nami originally had a giant battle-axe as her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:01.360
signature weapon instead of a bō and was going to&nbsp;
be a cyborg. The adorable Tony Tony Chopper was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:05.920
originally a more realistic reindeer who walked&nbsp;
on two legs and smoked. In an interview with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:10.560
Shōnen Jump, Eiichiro Oda said the reason why&nbsp;
he changed Chopper's original appearance is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.520
blended too much with the other characters, so he&nbsp;
made Chopper more distinct, cute, and small. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.640
former pirate hunter Roronoa Zoro was envisioned&nbsp;
as the bodyguard for Buggy the Star Clown. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.880
chain-smoking cook Sanji was going to be named&nbsp;
Naruto, however, with the "Naruto" manga about to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:32.200
start serialization, Oda wisely changed the name&nbsp;
to avoid any confusion between the two characters.

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:36.480
The fantasy world of "One Piece” might be a work&nbsp;
of fiction, but many of the series' outlandish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.880
characters are inspired by real-life pirates from&nbsp;
the 17th century. Gol D. Roger, arguably the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:46.200
important character in "One Piece," shares&nbsp;
inspiration with two real-life pirates. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:52.240
first is the French pirate Olivier Levasseur who,&nbsp;
upon his hanging, tossed a cryptogram from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.400
scaffolding into the crowd and told them if they&nbsp;
could figure out the message his treasure would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.920
be theirs. Levasseur's treasure was never found.&nbsp;
Similarly, Gol D. Roger tells pirates everywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.840
to go look for his treasure, the One Piece, before&nbsp;
he's executed by the World Government. The second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:12.120
source of inspiration for Gol D. Roger is Henry&nbsp;
Every, an English pirate who led possibly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:18.200
most profitable pirate raid of all time in 1695.&nbsp;
After capturing a treasure estimated at around $95&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:24.120
million in today's currency, Every became known&nbsp;
as "The Arch Pirate" for his tremendous wealth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:27.880
There are other examples of real-life pirate&nbsp;
references in "One Piece," such as the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:32.800
"Blackbeard," Edward Teach inspiring Marshall D.&nbsp;
Teach, leader of the Blackbeard Pirates. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:38.120
also the Welsh pirate Bartholomew Roberts serving&nbsp;
as inspiration for Bartholomew Kuma , the legend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:44.520
of Awilda for "Iron Mace" Alvida, and the Irish&nbsp;
pirate Anne Bonny for Captain Jewelry Bonney.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:49.240
Easter eggs in anime are pretty common, and&nbsp;
"One Piece" is no exception to the rule. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:54.120
of the most prominent and commonly deployed&nbsp;
Easter eggs of Eiichiro Oda is Pandaman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:59.440
an anthropomorphic panda wrestler he drew for a&nbsp;
"Kinnikuman" character creation contest when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:04.240
was a teenager, as he explained in Vol. 7 of&nbsp;
the "One Piece" manga. As a joke character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:08.280
Pandaman has been featured in various&nbsp;
materials such as anime, manga, movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:11.800
and video games. His lore even&nbsp;
unfolds in various backgrounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:16.600
as his love interest Pandawoman, his&nbsp;
angry unpaid creditors the Tomato Gang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:22.280
and his great rival the Unforgivable Mask&nbsp;
have all made cameos in Oda projects.

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:26.480
Even though "One Piece" gets away with a lot&nbsp;
more than your average kids' cartoon in the U.S,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:31.160
it still has a younger-skewing audience in&nbsp;
Japan. Therefore, the anime tends to water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:35.520
down the manga's most graphic moments. Case in&nbsp;
point — characters who give the middle finger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:40.320
in the manga do not do so in the anime. There&nbsp;
are various examples of characters flipping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:44.600
the bird to others in the manga, so much&nbsp;
so that it became a running gag. The one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:49.560
character most associated with this offensive&nbsp;
gesture is Trafalgar D. Water Law. When Law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:54.360
meets Eustass Kid at the Human Auctioning House,&nbsp;
he gives him the middle finger. In the anime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:59.680
Law simply shoots Kid a smug grin. When&nbsp;
Donquixote Doflamingo defeats Law in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:04.320
"Dressrosa" arc of the manga, Law gives him the&nbsp;
middle finger in defiance. The anime version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:10.040
dulls the edge of this rebellious moment by&nbsp;
having Law verbally mock Doflamingo instead.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:14.080
There's no question that the works of&nbsp;
"Dragon Ball" and its ilk played a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:18.160
role in the development of "One Piece," but&nbsp;
there's one obscure series that doesn't get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:22.400
enough credit for inspiring Eiichiro Oda.&nbsp;
As he's stated in numerous interviews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:27.920
Oda became fascinated with the world of pirates&nbsp;
thanks to a little 1974 cartoon called "Vicky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:33.440
the Viking." The show — which aired in Austria,&nbsp;
Germany, and Japan — follows a boy named Vicky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:38.640
who is the son of Halvar, chief of the Viking&nbsp;
village of Flake. Despite being feeble, shy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:42.840
and terrified of wolves, Vicky manages&nbsp;
to get himself and his fellow Vikings out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:47.440
of the toughest situations thanks to his&nbsp;
sharp imagination. Interestingly enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:52.480
"One Piece" isn't the only anime inspired by&nbsp;
"Vicky the Viking." Makoto Yukimura, the writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.440
and illustrator of "Vinland Saga," has also cited&nbsp;
"Vicky the Viking" as a source of inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:02.680
and even drew some of the main characters&nbsp;
from the kids' show as cameos in his manga.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:08.440
The "One Piece" TV anime series from Toei&nbsp;
Animation has been on the air since 1999.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:12.360
During that time, the show has undergone&nbsp;
various changes in its animation style.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:17.520
The recent adaptation of the "Wano Country" story&nbsp;
arc, for example, has experienced some significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:23.320
changes in the show's art style. Of course, change&nbsp;
isn't always a bad thing. Episode 1015 of "One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:28.120
Piece" was praised as one of the most visually&nbsp;
stunning episodes in the series history. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:32.280
not everyone is on board with the recent&nbsp;
stylistic changes to the show. A few fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:36.760
have harassed animator Henry Thurlow online,&nbsp;
believing him to be the cause of the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:41.600
controversial use of power auras that recall&nbsp;
fight scenes from "Dragon Ball Z." To his credit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:46.320
Thurlow took it in stride and poked fun at the&nbsp;
thought of any single animator having so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.040
creative control over a mega-franchise&nbsp;
like "One Piece." There have also been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:54.760
"One Piece" fans who have apologized for the&nbsp;
harassment Thurlow has received and offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:59.880
their support. On the One Piece Podcast, Thurlow&nbsp;
clarified the anime's use of auras, stating that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:06.160
the decision of when to use it and by how much&nbsp;
is decided by the individual episode directors.

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:09.640
Thanks to the work of Funimation (now&nbsp;
known as Crunchyroll), the "One Piece"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:14.360
anime series has experienced a resurgence in&nbsp;
popularity in North America. The series is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:19.000
widely available in its original form, subbed&nbsp;
and dubbed, on various streaming platforms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:23.880
in digital stores, and home media releases.&nbsp;
Films like "One Piece: Stampede" have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:27.960
rolled out at North American theaters to&nbsp;
commercial success, with "One Piece Film:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:33.160
Red" making its U.S. debut on November 4th of&nbsp;
2022. North American "One Piece" fans have it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:38.000
good. That wasn't always the case, however, as&nbsp;
the defunct 4Kids Entertainment originally owned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.640
the English rights to the "One Piece" anime and&nbsp;
subjected it to countless questionable changes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:47.680
Like most 4Kids dubs of its era, "One Piece"&nbsp;
was heavily censored to ridiculous degrees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:52.400
The 4Kids dubs were Americanized like crazy&nbsp;
and had most of its original music replaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:56.880
by an interchangeable track. Worst of all,&nbsp;
some episodes of "One Piece" were skipped or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:01.840
merged into a singular episode. Needless to&nbsp;
say, fans were not happy with these changes.

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:06.440
The lasting legacy of the 4Kids dub of "One&nbsp;
Piece," however, is its American intro that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:11.600
tells the story of the Straw Hat Pirates through&nbsp;
a reasonably corny rap song. Oddly enough, 4Kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:16.880
also produced an English cover of "We Are!," the&nbsp;
first "One Piece" anime opening song but decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:21.720
against using it. While hardly anyone mourns the&nbsp;
loss of 4Kids and their rights to "One Piece," the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:26.760
fandom has embraced the 4Kids "One Piece" intro&nbsp;
music. After all, how many other classic anime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:32.440
series can say they've got a rap theme song? And&nbsp;
who can hate a line like "Yo-ho-ho he took a bite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:38.320
of Gum Gum!" Shawn Freshco Conrad, the man behind&nbsp;
the rap, explained how the theme song came to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:43.440
He even updated the rap for the crew members&nbsp;
that don't get to appear in the 4Kids dub.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:48.320
Who says Monkey D. Luffy doesn't have class? While&nbsp;
everyone's favorite straw hat-wearing hero hasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:53.800
become king of the pirates just yet, he has set an&nbsp;
unexpected milestone in the real world. He became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:58.560
the first anime character to grace the cover&nbsp;
of Shueisha's Men's Non-No fashion magazine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:04.760
in 2009. This is an amazing feat considering&nbsp;
the publication's 24-year history at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:10.880
It also marked the first time Eiichiro Oda had&nbsp;
drawn a cover image for a non-manga magazine.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:14.440
Various aspects of "One Piece" have&nbsp;
been influenced by historical figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:19.240
and real-life events. When designing the three&nbsp;
significant marine admirals from the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:24.200
Eiichiro Oda turned to three late but great&nbsp;
Japanese actors as a source of inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:29.320
for their appearances. Kuzan is based on the&nbsp;
late Japanese actor Yūsaku Matsuda who starred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:33.760
in films like "The Beast to Die," "Detective&nbsp;
Story," and "Black Rain." Matsuda's status as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:38.720
an actor is so legendary that he also serves&nbsp;
as inspiration for Kenshiro from "Fist of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:44.280
the North Star" and Spike Spiegel from "Cowboy&nbsp;
Bebop." Borsalino is based on the late Japanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:49.400
actor Kunie Tanaka who appeared in films like&nbsp;
"The Bad Sleep Well," "Sanjuro," and "Battles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:56.520
Without Honor and Humanity." Finally, Sakazuki is&nbsp;
based on the late Japanese actor Bunta Sugawara.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:59.800
As "One Piece" is hardly above&nbsp;
promoting products now and again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:03.840
a series of "One Piece" noodle commercials&nbsp;
reimagined the cast as modern-day high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:07.360
schoolers. The advertising campaign&nbsp;
was called "Hungry Days" and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:12.120
for the Japanese food company Nissin Foods&nbsp;
to advertise their lineup of instant noodles.

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:16.280
The modern-day designs of the Straw Hat&nbsp;
Pirates were drawn by Eisaku Kubonouchi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:21.360
("Carole &amp; Tuesday") and animated by Shaft&nbsp;
Animation Studio. In the ad campaign that ran from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:27.080
May 2019 to February 2020, each commercial focused&nbsp;
on a crew member that evoked emotional moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:32.160
from the series related to that crew member, only&nbsp;
reinterpreted to fit the present-day high school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:37.760
theme. The first commercial had Roronoa Zoro&nbsp;
swearing to get stronger after losing a kendo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:42.280
match to Mihawk. The second has Nami being&nbsp;
overwhelmed by her high school studies and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:47.440
evening work at the Arlong Pirates' restaurant.&nbsp;
The third commercial has Nefertari Vivi recalling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:51.560
precious memories of her high school friends&nbsp;
(the Straw Hat crew) as she transfers to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:55.800
new school. The fourth is an all-out brawl&nbsp;
between the White Team (Whitebeard Pirates)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.800 --> 00:14:02.480
and Red Team (Marines) in an event involving&nbsp;
Monkey D. Luffy and his brother Portgas D. Ace.

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:06.200
One of the great things about the "One Piece"&nbsp;
series is the communication between fans and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:10.360
series creator Eiichiro Oda. Through the&nbsp;
question-and-answer column in the manga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:14.640
Oda often answers fans' lingering questions&nbsp;
about the world of "One Piece," no matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:19.720
how ridiculous the questions may be. For&nbsp;
Volume 56 of the question-and-answer column,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:24.000
there was one fan who had a more serious&nbsp;
question: "If One Piece was set in the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:28.120
world, which countries would the nine&nbsp;
Straw Hats come from?" Oda's response:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:32.360
"Well. I'll just put what fits with the&nbsp;
character's image." Here's what Oda put down:

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:33.560
"Luffy: Brazil

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:34.880
Zoro: Japan

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:36.120
Nami: Sweden

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:37.320
Usopp: Africa

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:38.600
Sanji: France

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:39.920
Chopper: Canada

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:41.080
Robin: Russia

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:42.480
Franky: USA

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:44.000
Brook: Austria[.]"

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:47.480
Obviously, Africa is a continent&nbsp;
and not a country. Whether or not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:50.280
Oda has an extremely limited&nbsp;
knowledge of global geography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:55.000
or simply didn't think hard enough about&nbsp;
this particular fan question is unknown.

00:14:55.000 --> 00:15:00.400
Considering the love and admiration Eiichiro&nbsp;
Oda and Akira Toriyama have for one another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:05.040
it was inevitable that the two manga creators&nbsp;
would work together on a crossover involving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:08.640
their popular series. And keeping&nbsp;
true to their wacky sense of humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:14.160
the highly anticipated "One Piece x Dragon Ball"&nbsp;
crossover was anything but normal. "Cross Epoch,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:19.240
takes the cast of "One Piece" and "Dragon Ball"&nbsp;
and puts them in a far-out story where Mr. Satan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:25.040
invites everyone to a little tea party after using&nbsp;
the Dragon Balls to wish to be king. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:29.440
the "Dragon Ball" and "One Piece" crews all make&nbsp;
it on time and have fun. Some of the characters'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:34.120
appearances and occupations are changed to better&nbsp;
mirror one another — Bulma becomes a thief to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:39.600
match Nami, Piccolo masters sword fighting to&nbsp;
reflect Roronoa Zoro. This isn't the only time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:43.680
"One Piece" and "Dragon Ball" have crossed over&nbsp;
with each other, as they often appear together in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:49.920
various Shōnen Jump-related games like "Battle&nbsp;
Stadium D.O.N" or the ill-fated "Jump Force."

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:55.080
According to series creator Eiichiro Oda,&nbsp;
"One Piece" is entering its final story arc,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:59.360
meaning the series' quarter-century journey will&nbsp;
soon come to an end. There's no telling where this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:03.520
ship will sail for its final destination, but&nbsp;
there's one thing that everyone can be certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:08.600
of — before the series is complete, at least one&nbsp;
character in "One Piece" will wear an eye patch.

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:13.120
Confused? In the over one thousand chapters&nbsp;
of "One Piece," there has not been a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:18.480
pirate with an eye patch. The same is true for&nbsp;
the anime TV series and movies. Why? According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:22.560
to Oda in the "One Piece 10th Treasures"&nbsp;
special collectors' book, he said he wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:27.080
to avoid the stereotypical pirate look. Oda&nbsp;
is not against the idea of drawing a pirate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:31.360
with an eye patch — he's just more interested&nbsp;
in the journey that gets them there. He wrote,

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:34.520
"I thought, if there's an image in&nbsp;
everyone's head about what a pirate is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:36.520
then I thought I would write [and] draw about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.520 --> 00:16:40.000
process of the boy getting there. But&nbsp;
it's not like I dislike eye patches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:44.160
so in the final scenes of One Piece, there&nbsp;
is one pirate who appears with an eye patch."

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:47.480
Fans have speculated that the fabled&nbsp;
eye-patch-wearing pirate character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:50.560
could be Monkey D. Luffy, as&nbsp;
the story of "One Piece" has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:54.000
been his journey from a young&nbsp;
dreamer to king of the pirates.

